it sees a river, it sees a farmland.

231
00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:50.040
because whether you read it

232
00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:54.160
as poem or code, it functions
as a set of executable instructions.

233
00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:56.000
This is her rallying cry

234
00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:59.800
to her people to build a future
that embraces the multitudes of blackness.

235
00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:03.720
And this future is built with a love
like black are black.

236
00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:07.880
And I think that this is the mantra
that Bertram bears out in a new sermon,

237
00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:07.920
Reading the landscape
as a prompt for reflection,

238
00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:11.280
because they show that they are conscious
of this relationality

239
00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:16.080
by remixing the legacies of their poetic
and algorithmic predecessors,

240
00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:19.520
because no one is doing anything in coding
without someone else.

241
00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:23.320
Teams of others, in fact,
and more importantly,

242
00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:26.600
Bertram is taking up this continuous work
by positioning

243
00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:29.680
themselves as an unimagined coder
in writing and coding.

244
00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:29.720
So coming up to near the end, very good.

245
00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:33.680
Travesty generator computing practices
have historically excluded

246
00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:38.040
people of color, women, and those from
lower socioeconomic backgrounds,

247
00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:38.080
I've had to be very schematic.

248
00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:42.400
and while there have been
increasing efforts at making computation

249
00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:46.200
a more accessible resource
for them, attaining algorithmic literacy

250
00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:50.520
remains a former privilege
that renders these individuals unimagined.

251
00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:54.720
Readers and coders of algorithms,
let alone black experimental code work,

252
00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:59.200
and by being explicit in their positioning
as an unimagined black programmer

253
00:10:59.200 --> 00:10:59.280
It's unlikely to achieve that aim, but
that's part of the esthetic aspirations,

254
00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:03.680
whose code confronts the oppressive
political reality of their people.

255
00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:03.760
this idea of imagining another world
and imagining another world.

256
00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:07.960
Bertram's work is also an intervention
into a literary

257
00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:11.920
and computational space
that has long excluded people of color,

258
00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:12.560
what are the challenges
involved in doing so?

259
00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.040
and I think that at its core,

260
00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:18.400
this intervention is motivated by love.

261
00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:18.480
quite important to me in terms of modeling
what another world would look like,

262
00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:22.840
Bertram's intention with programing
the unimagined coder into existence.

263
00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:23.440
what another sort of speculative sensory
landscape may ultimately resemble as well.

264
00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:24.440
And eventually what I hope to do
is because the thing

265
00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:25.480
It advocates

266
00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:28.520
for a new narrative
where there can be more black coders

267
00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:33.120
and poets experimenting with this literary
and computational practice.

268
00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:33.320
I'd love to be able to do performances
with this apparatus.

269
00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:34.880
What Gwendolyn Brooks cause

270
00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:37.920
a lot like black are black
is exemplified by their commitment

271
00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:41.320
to amplifying the voices
and realities of the black community.

272
00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:41.360
We can annotate the data
that it's generating in real time,

273
00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:44.840
And sort of code is something
that has meaning beyond its functionality

274
00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:48.200
as a form of symbolic expression
and interaction.

275
00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:52.360
Then it can be said that love,
specifically a love embodied in honoring

276
00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:57.480
once lineages and inheriting the work,
inheriting the work of community advocacy.

277
00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:01.520
That's what Bertram's code is expressing
and interacting with.

278
00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:01.640
Thank you very much for listening.

279
00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:05.640
And finally, I would like to end
with this quote from Jacob Brown,

280
00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:07.800
who says, when I say I love you,

281
00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:10.880
I mean for you to understand
that I exist in relation to you.

282
00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:14.560
And I really like this quote,
because I think it perfectly encapsulates

283
00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:19.320
the relationality that strategy and
Bertram bear out in their home generators.

284
00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:21.320
And ultimately,
I think that there is no greater

285
00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:25.720
act of love than centering your craft
around your communities and relations,

286
00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:30.040
despite the political circumstances
that threaten to sever these ties.

287
00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:35.640
This is my list of works,
and that is all I have for you today.

288
00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:37.040
Thank you very much.
